Comment:
1) Schedule F carries the risk of unwarranted intrusion on merit-based review of science thereby limiting the ability to conduct science without bias.
2) Peer review for NIH & other agencies is mandated by federal law;
3) Program officers at NIH, DOD, NSF & other agencies should not become Schedule F employees.

This is a blatant attempt to subvert the will of the people and make science weak:

1) Schedule F carries the risk of unwarranted intrusion on merit-based review of science;
2) Peer review for NIH & other agencies is mandated by federal law;
3) Program officers at NIH, DOD, NSF & other agencies should not become Schedule F employees.

The Benefits section asserts that faster removals will create “large gains in agency efficiency” but admits OPM “does not have a reasonable basis for estimating the magnitude.” E.O. 12866 requires the agency to quantify benefits where feasible.  At minimum, OPM can draw on:

  - MSPB survey data on supervisor time lost to poor performers;  
  - FDIC Special Counsel report quantifying misconduct-related costs;  
  - GAO’s 2015 estimate ($350 million/yr) of “desk-duty” employees.

Why were these readily available sources ignored?  Absent monetized benefits, OPM cannot show the rule is cost-justified, nor that it qualifies as an E.O. 14192 “deregulatory action.”  The final rule must supply concrete dollar values or explain, with record evidence, why monetization is impossible.

Greater federal workforce turnover, with changes in presidential administrations ushering in new cadres of policy-influencing employees, causing increased instability in agencies’ interpretations and implementations of laws;
Decline in the institutional history and technical expertise career civil servants develop and bring to bear in formulating policy;
Less accountability to Congress regarding the laws that agencies administer; and
More opportunities for political favoritism in federal contracting.

To comply with the law and avoid further undermining whistleblower protections, OPM must:  
1.Rescind the rule immediately, or  
2. Issue a binding certification stating that:
   - No provision of this rule may be used to investigate, discipline, or remove employees for lawful whistleblowing;  
   - Any adverse action taken under this rule that follows a protected disclosure will be presumed retaliatory unless proven otherwise by clear and convincing evidence;  
   - Employees disciplined under this rule retain full rights under the Whistleblower Protection Act and **5 U.S.C. § 2302(b) (8)
